  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
our General Public Licenses are intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make it free software for
all its users.

  This license, the GNU Affero General Public License, applies to certain
specifically designated software projects--typically open-source software
projects--that are hosted and run on servers. It is designed to ensure
that anyone who interacts with the software over a network has the freedom
to access the source code.

  The licenses for most software are designed to take away your freedom to
share and change the works.  By contrast, our General Public Licenses are
intended to guarantee your freedom to share and change all versions of a
program--to make it free software for all its users.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not price.
Our General Public Licenses are designed to make free all software--to
make it free to use, to change, and to share it.  At the same time, we
protect our rights by prohibiting others from making unauthorized copies
or claiming that they are the authors of our work.

  However, there is an important distinction between general software and
network server software.  The latter is often used in business
environments where users interact with the software over a network. The
GNU Affero General Public License addresses this by requiring that anyone
who modifies the software and runs it as a network service must make the
source code available to the users of that service.

  The following license terms apply to the open-source portion of this
software. For commercial use, a separate commercial license must be
obtained from 深圳市中科劲纬智能有限公司.

